here's an interesting story for economic  context it's really important when we  talk about how much things cost in  government in politics in society it's  important to consider what other things  cost in government as well so for  example like when we hear a proposal for  Medicare for all and we hear a dollar  figure for what it would cost those  dollar figures are so huge that they can  be meaningless to us and it's sort of  like thinking about a time duration of a  million years how do we as humans  conceive of the time duration a million  years it's really difficult when you  consider the lifespan of the average  humans so when we compare Medicare from  when we look at Medicare for all we  should also consider well what do we  already spend on health care what do we  spend on the military we need context in  order to really understand the economic  price tag that exists for programs so  when we talk about the cost of Obama  care or the cost of trumps tariffs it's  useful to have analogies and other data  and there happens to be research from  National Taxpayers Union Foundation  which points out that this latest round  of Donald Trump's tariffs have made it  so that the total increase in cost to  Americans for Trump's tariffs are  greater than the average per person  taxes for Obama care okay  the cost of the enacted tariffs before  the additional tariffs that will start  on January 1st of 2019 will be about 42  billion dollars the cost of the  Affordable Care Act it's going to be 34  billion dollars next year the tariffs  will cost Americans more than Obama care  which we were told is too expensive and  we can't afford afford this is what many  people including Trump don't understand  about tariffs Trump talks about putting  tariffs on other countries the tariffs  are paid for by the business is  importing for those countries so when  you say we are going to put a tariff on  China for goods that they export to the  United States  it's the businesses buying those goods  here that actually pay for that that  means layoffs that means moving  manufacturing out of the u.s. that means  finding ways to circumvent the law to  avoid the tariffs and since we're  talking about tariffs this is also a  good time to talk about the  trade deficit yesterday and earlier  today we talked about the budget deficit  Donald Trump successfully framed himself  to his supporters as an expert on trade  who knew how to fix it and in doing so  Trump talked about how the trade deficit  was too high and trade deficit means  when you import more than you export you  have a trade deficit when you export  more than you import you have a trade  surplus Donald Trump has been messing  with these tariffs for several months  now and lo and behold the trade deficit  has increased during this time to a  six-month high soybean shipments keep  declining imports keep climbing meaning  not only are Trump's tariffs not working  to reduce the trade deficit but American  companies are spending more money on  foreign goods rather than redirecting  that money domestically if you are an  economic nationalist and most people are  like economic nationalism is mostly  unquestioned among the left and right  although there are people in our  audience who I know disagree economic  nationalism means that you believe the  economic policy of the US for example  should be geared towards helping the  u.s. and Americans and American  companies that's what Donald Trump  claims to be doing economic nationalism  the policy is failing by the standard of  economic nationalism and we know Trump  comes from the business world he's  running America like a business the  problem is that many of trumps  businesses went bankrupt and the  employees lost their jobs if you're a  supporter of the tariffs and I know that  there are people in the audience who are  please get in touch and tell me when  will the tariffs start having a positive  impact because so far the tariffs are  costing Americans 